<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Offa of Mercia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 796</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Offa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Offa was king of Mercia in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> from 757 to 796 and the most powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> king before Alfred. He brought Kent, Sussex and East Anglia under his rule, crushed Kentish resistance and dealt with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> as an equal. He is remembered for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offas-dyke">Offa's Dyke</a>, the earthwork along the Welsh border, and for a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> penny coinage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia.jpg" alt="Offa of Mercia" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Offa (07..-0796; roi de Mercie). Autorité émettrice de monnaie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Offa,_King_of_Mercia,_silver_penny;_(obverse).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
